  "live_records": {
    "q-35828": {
      "dom_id": "step-q35828",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\frac{3x+4}{6-15x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+4}{6-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{15x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-15)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0}{0-15} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{1}{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
