    "q-68043": {
      "dom_id": "step-q68043",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+x}{2{x}^{2}+x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{x})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
