      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+1}{{x}^{3}-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}+1}{{x}^{3}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+0)}{(1-0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
