    "q-339615": {
      "dom_id": "step-q339615",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{7}+3}{{x}^{4}+6x+2}=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{7}+3}{{x}^{4}+6x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{7}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}{(1+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{3}+0}{1+0+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
